WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.615 --> 00:00:04.888
- What's up Believe Nation?

00:00:04.888 --> 00:00:06.657
I started the Mentor
Me Series with the goal

00:00:06.657 --> 00:00:09.325
to try to learn from people
who've done a lot more than us,

00:00:09.325 --> 00:00:11.543
hang around them a little bit longer,

00:00:11.543 --> 00:00:13.277
and by being in their environment,

00:00:13.277 --> 00:00:15.377
hopefully some of their
mindsets, their beliefs,

00:00:15.377 --> 00:00:17.555
their way of thinking, seep into us

00:00:17.555 --> 00:00:20.877
to help us become the
best version of ourselves.

00:00:20.877 --> 00:00:23.102
So today, we're going to
learn from Kevin O'Leary

00:00:23.102 --> 00:00:24.872
and his investing rules.

00:00:24.872 --> 00:00:26.226
Mentor Me Kevin.

00:00:26.226 --> 00:00:27.788
And as always guys, as
you're watching the clip,

00:00:27.788 --> 00:00:29.348
if you hear something that
really really resonates

00:00:29.348 --> 00:00:31.614
with you, please leave it
down in the comments below

00:00:31.614 --> 00:00:33.795
and put quotes around it so other people

00:00:33.795 --> 00:00:35.770
can be inspired, and also
when you write it down,

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:38.811
you're much more likely to
lock it in for yourself too.

00:00:38.811 --> 00:00:39.644
Enjoy!

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:44.117
(inspirational music)

00:00:52.799 --> 00:00:56.544
- Diversification is the
only free lunch in investing,

00:00:56.544 --> 00:00:58.313
and I see this happen so many times.

00:00:58.313 --> 00:01:00.619
Here's my basic lesson that I've learned

00:01:00.619 --> 00:01:02.474
that I never vary from.

00:01:02.474 --> 00:01:04.613
And this works.
Trust me, it works.

00:01:04.613 --> 00:01:07.534
Never more than five
percent of your portfolio

00:01:07.534 --> 00:01:10.907
in any one name, no matter
how great the story is.

00:01:10.907 --> 00:01:14.156
If it's the next Nortel, if
it's the next Rim, who cares.

00:01:14.156 --> 00:01:16.135
Five percent max waiting.

00:01:16.135 --> 00:01:18.278
For me, usually it's
two-and-a-half to three percent.

00:01:18.278 --> 00:01:21.529
I never let a stock get bigger
than that in my portfolio.

00:01:21.529 --> 00:01:23.824
I sell into the strength all the time.

00:01:23.824 --> 00:01:27.991
Secondly, never, ever, ever
more than 20% in any one sector.

00:01:29.212 --> 00:01:31.809
So if you love gold, you
just can't get past 20%

00:01:31.809 --> 00:01:35.175
of your net worth in gold, or
energy, or any other sector,

00:01:35.175 --> 00:01:36.540
or financial services,

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:40.122
so good sectoral
diversification is crucial.

00:01:40.122 --> 00:01:43.573
And finally, for me, I'm over 60% weighted

00:01:43.573 --> 00:01:45.753
in debt now versus equity.

00:01:45.753 --> 00:01:47.970
Not government debt, not treasury bonds,

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:49.948
but corporate debt.

00:01:49.948 --> 00:01:52.331
Remember that a double-B or triple-B bond

00:01:52.331 --> 00:01:54.753
is serviced before they pay
the dividend on a common stock,

00:01:54.753 --> 00:01:57.264
so I like to own right
up the balance sheet,

00:01:57.264 --> 00:02:00.147
so I own a lot of prefs,
converts, senior debt,

00:02:00.147 --> 00:02:02.822
floating rate notes, I love that stuff.

00:02:02.822 --> 00:02:04.835
Because when they get upgraded,
I get capital appreciation.

00:02:04.835 --> 00:02:06.890
Meanwhile, I get a check.

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:09.313
The key to life is the check coming in.

00:02:09.313 --> 00:02:10.542
It's the cash.

00:02:10.542 --> 00:02:14.081
If you always, always, always
look at the free cash flow,

00:02:14.081 --> 00:02:16.310
this will keep you safe,
because the only friend

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:18.248
you're going to have when
you're old and crusty

00:02:18.248 --> 00:02:20.750
is not your dog, not your
kids, it's the cash in the bank

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:23.680
that's still going to love you.

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:25.858
- In your book, you also
say when you're investing,

00:02:25.858 --> 00:02:28.616
you should have an
element of fear in there.

00:02:28.616 --> 00:02:30.802
- You should make the assumption when you

00:02:30.802 --> 00:02:33.105
make an investment, there's
some probability you'll lose it.

00:02:33.105 --> 00:02:34.667
It'll go to zero.

00:02:34.667 --> 00:02:38.291
And that's why you need to
have, you know, a reserve,

00:02:38.291 --> 00:02:42.408
like a backup plan, some cash set aside,

00:02:42.408 --> 00:02:45.045
that if everything blows up, you're okay.

00:02:45.045 --> 00:02:47.224
I find it extraordinary when people

00:02:47.224 --> 00:02:50.265
make their first liquidity event,

00:02:50.265 --> 00:02:53.308
and they have some capital,
that they blow it all again.

00:02:53.308 --> 00:02:54.951
That is the biggest mistake.

00:02:54.951 --> 00:02:57.745
You've got to take a nut,
even if it stays in cash,

00:02:57.745 --> 00:03:00.339
or it's a very very liquid safe security,

00:03:00.339 --> 00:03:02.230
to say that I don't touch.

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:03.872
It goes back to what my mother taught me.

00:03:03.872 --> 00:03:07.037
Spend the interest, never the principal.

00:03:07.037 --> 00:03:08.969
I mean look, you know, I make
a lot of crazy investments.

00:03:08.969 --> 00:03:10.869
I don't touch the principal.

00:03:10.869 --> 00:03:13.589
I learned something a long
time ago from my mother.

00:03:13.589 --> 00:03:15.273
You're going to find this fascinating.

00:03:15.273 --> 00:03:17.284
When I was a seven-year-old boy,

00:03:17.284 --> 00:03:18.768
and she was working at that time,

00:03:18.768 --> 00:03:21.643
she used to take me to the
bank with her every Thursday.

00:03:21.643 --> 00:03:23.578
That's when they paid the
girls where she worked.

00:03:23.578 --> 00:03:25.267
And she'd take a third of her paycheck

00:03:25.267 --> 00:03:29.583
and put it into bonds and
dividend-paying stocks.

00:03:29.583 --> 00:03:31.846
She used to say to my brother and I then,

00:03:31.846 --> 00:03:35.260
boys, never spend the
principal, only the interest.

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:37.930
I had no idea what she was talking about,

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:40.358
but she was drilling that
into my head for a reason,

00:03:40.358 --> 00:03:42.458
I think, became very
important decades later.

00:03:42.458 --> 00:03:46.526
After she passed away, I became
the executor for her estate.

00:03:46.526 --> 00:03:49.156
For the first time, I saw a secret account

00:03:49.156 --> 00:03:51.949
she kept from both of her
husbands her whole life,

00:03:51.949 --> 00:03:54.711
made up of dividend-paying
large cap stocks

00:03:54.711 --> 00:03:56.893
and corporate credit.

00:03:56.893 --> 00:04:01.328
Do you have any idea of the
performance of that portfolio?

00:04:01.328 --> 00:04:03.753
She blew away every indices.

00:04:03.753 --> 00:04:05.439
I was amazed.

00:04:05.439 --> 00:04:06.792
She wasn't a portfolio manager.

00:04:06.792 --> 00:04:09.956
She was just a concerned mother
that needed to have capital

00:04:09.956 --> 00:04:11.932
no matter what happened.

00:04:11.932 --> 00:04:13.826
I started to do some research.

00:04:13.826 --> 00:04:15.709
You may find this fascinating.

00:04:15.709 --> 00:04:20.102
Over the last 40 years, over
70% of the market's returns

00:04:20.102 --> 00:04:24.459
have come from dividends,
not capital appreciation.

00:04:24.459 --> 00:04:27.629
She must have intuitively
felt that and knew that.

00:04:27.629 --> 00:04:30.754
That forever changed my
investment philosophy.

00:04:30.754 --> 00:04:32.807
I'm a dividend guy.

00:04:32.807 --> 00:04:35.445
I only buy stocks that pay dividends.

00:04:35.445 --> 00:04:38.196
I'll never buy a stock that
doesn't return capital,

00:04:38.196 --> 00:04:40.621
because the data tells you not to.

00:04:40.621 --> 00:04:43.129
Unless you're a masterful stock picker,

00:04:43.129 --> 00:04:45.432
and you know exactly when to buy and sell,

00:04:45.432 --> 00:04:48.187
you can't, in my view, beat those odds.

00:04:48.187 --> 00:04:50.208
40 years, 71% returns.

00:04:50.208 --> 00:04:52.435
That's it for me, dividends only.

00:04:52.435 --> 00:04:55.106
I like gold because in
a way it's a stabilizer.

00:04:55.106 --> 00:04:56.835
It's an insurance policy.

00:04:56.835 --> 00:04:59.542
I listen to all the gold pundits,

00:04:59.542 --> 00:05:01.438
and they're always wrong
as far as I'm concerned.

00:05:01.438 --> 00:05:03.127
It's impossible to time the moves.

00:05:03.127 --> 00:05:04.855
I've owned gold for decades,

00:05:04.855 --> 00:05:07.203
and I simply have a five percent waiting.

00:05:07.203 --> 00:05:08.768
I look at it quarterly.

00:05:08.768 --> 00:05:10.414
When it becomes more than five percent,

00:05:10.414 --> 00:05:12.221
I sell into the strength,

00:05:12.221 --> 00:05:14.359
and when it weakens, I
buy into the weakness.

00:05:14.359 --> 00:05:15.920
It's just a stabilizer.

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:17.772
I'm not one of those guys

00:05:17.772 --> 00:05:19.662
that's going to go to 40% waiting in gold.

00:05:19.662 --> 00:05:20.495
- Okay.

00:05:20.495 --> 00:05:21.729
- I don't believe the
hyperinflation story.

00:05:21.729 --> 00:05:22.562
It hasn't happened.

00:05:22.562 --> 00:05:24.489
Gold is popular for a
whole host of reasons.

00:05:24.489 --> 00:05:25.442
- [Interviewer] Yes.

00:05:25.442 --> 00:05:27.374
- And five percent seems to be enough.

00:05:27.374 --> 00:05:29.432
It's worked for me in
portfolio management.

00:05:29.432 --> 00:05:32.147
It's the only security I own
that doesn't pay a dividend.

00:05:32.147 --> 00:05:35.358
- So, Kevin, I guess,
do you not care if gold

00:05:35.358 --> 00:05:38.034
goes to 2,000 or 5,000 dollars an ounce?

00:05:38.034 --> 00:05:39.477
You're still going to buy gold?

00:05:39.477 --> 00:05:40.587
- Yeah, I don't care.

00:05:40.587 --> 00:05:41.740
Like let it run.

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:43.099
I'll be selling into that strength,

00:05:43.099 --> 00:05:44.786
keeping at five percent waiting.

00:05:44.786 --> 00:05:46.183
What I've learned.

00:05:46.183 --> 00:05:47.790
I'm an old crusty investor.

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:49.804
When everybody is saying
you got to own it,

00:05:49.804 --> 00:05:51.042
you should be selling it,

00:05:51.042 --> 00:05:52.279
because when it corrects,

00:05:52.279 --> 00:05:53.837
it doesn't touch the
sides on the way down.

00:05:53.837 --> 00:05:55.932
So I simply say five
percent's good for me.

00:05:55.932 --> 00:05:57.990
Some people like it 10, 15% waiting.

00:05:57.990 --> 00:05:59.262
I'm a five percent guy.

00:05:59.262 --> 00:06:00.739
And I'm disciplined about it.

00:06:00.739 --> 00:06:02.184
I hope it runs to 5,000.

00:06:02.184 --> 00:06:03.376
I don't care.

00:06:03.376 --> 00:06:04.814
I'll be selling, selling,
selling, selling,

00:06:04.814 --> 00:06:06.417
all the way up, keeping
it at five percent,

00:06:06.417 --> 00:06:08.111
and when it corrects back down to 2,000,

00:06:08.111 --> 00:06:10.250
I'll be buying it in.
It's fun.

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:11.850
That's the way you should work with gold.

00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:13.741
Never get so caught up that you take

00:06:13.741 --> 00:06:16.509
all your dividend-yielding securities

00:06:16.509 --> 00:06:18.617
that are also a hedge against inflation

00:06:18.617 --> 00:06:20.922
and put it into a commodity like gold.

00:06:20.922 --> 00:06:24.051
What's the value of a stock
that never returns capital

00:06:24.051 --> 00:06:26.686
to its shareholders?
I don't know.

00:06:26.686 --> 00:06:28.537
Because the only way you can make money

00:06:28.537 --> 00:06:31.094
is if somebody else is
willing to buy that position

00:06:31.094 --> 00:06:35.119
at a higher price for some
emotional reason perhaps,

00:06:35.119 --> 00:06:38.773
or for some foresight
that maybe the company

00:06:38.773 --> 00:06:41.018
will return capital one day,

00:06:41.018 --> 00:06:44.281
and I think, what you learn as an investor

00:06:44.281 --> 00:06:48.550
over multiple decades is
the only thing that matters

00:06:48.550 --> 00:06:51.142
is free cash flow.
That's it.

00:06:51.142 --> 00:06:53.936
There is no other reason to own a stock.

00:06:53.936 --> 00:06:57.844
And with that philosophy, it
brings in you into a place

00:06:57.844 --> 00:07:01.882
where you focus on a
company's ability to generate

00:07:01.882 --> 00:07:04.930
incremental cash flow, because just owning

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:06.787
a dividend-paying stock
is not good enough,

00:07:06.787 --> 00:07:09.925
because let's say we find
a stock today that's paying

00:07:09.925 --> 00:07:12.312
a three percent dividend
yield and tomorrow,

00:07:12.312 --> 00:07:14.988
because its forecast for
sales get cut in half,

00:07:14.988 --> 00:07:17.783
the stock drops by 50%, now
it's yielding six percent.

00:07:17.783 --> 00:07:20.004
I don't want to own that stock either,

00:07:20.004 --> 00:07:23.463
so my test in this index that I've created

00:07:23.463 --> 00:07:26.500
with FTSE Russell looks
at the balance sheet.

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:30.628
Every year we test to make
sure that the company is viable

00:07:30.628 --> 00:07:33.203
in its ability to generate cash.

00:07:33.203 --> 00:07:35.589
This is extremely conservative investing.

00:07:35.589 --> 00:07:37.643
This is for the long haul.

00:07:37.643 --> 00:07:39.788
These tools are not for,
as you're suggesting,

00:07:39.788 --> 00:07:43.338
for spicy, you know,
the hot stock du jour.

00:07:43.338 --> 00:07:45.893
I've done that.
I've been there.

00:07:45.893 --> 00:07:48.143
Let the young legs do that.

00:07:49.088 --> 00:07:50.490
I have zero interest in that.

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:52.223
I don't care what the hot new stock is.

00:07:52.223 --> 00:07:56.292
When a company comes public,
I won't own it either.

00:07:56.292 --> 00:07:58.886
It's got to prove to
me over multiple years

00:07:58.886 --> 00:08:00.858
that it can continue to generate cash

00:08:00.858 --> 00:08:03.696
before it even fits into what I'm doing,

00:08:03.696 --> 00:08:06.374
so I'm really boring,
and I like it that way.

00:08:06.374 --> 00:08:08.056
- So a great company doesn't necessarily

00:08:08.056 --> 00:08:09.614
have to equal a great stock.

00:08:09.614 --> 00:08:11.751
They can be two very different
things from your perspective.

00:08:11.751 --> 00:08:13.683
- There's many companies
where I buy their products

00:08:13.683 --> 00:08:16.271
and services, I would
never touch their stock.

00:08:16.271 --> 00:08:20.427
So it's sort of, we're
talking about real money here,

00:08:20.427 --> 00:08:23.413
the stuff that you need to preserve.

00:08:23.413 --> 00:08:27.461
When I think about my
family trust, I can't afford

00:08:27.461 --> 00:08:29.309
to mess around with that.

00:08:29.309 --> 00:08:32.308
- What investments are you liking today?

00:08:32.308 --> 00:08:36.259
- I'm liking very simple
deals where I can understand

00:08:36.259 --> 00:08:37.369
the business model.

00:08:37.369 --> 00:08:39.265
I'll give you an example
of how boring and simple

00:08:39.265 --> 00:08:40.991
and how successful it can be.

00:08:40.991 --> 00:08:43.003
This is from Shark Tank.

00:08:43.003 --> 00:08:46.618
I look at a lot of deals on
Shark Tank and on Dragon's Den,

00:08:46.618 --> 00:08:48.344
because I invest five
percent of my net worth

00:08:48.344 --> 00:08:51.149
every year in venture deals.

00:08:51.149 --> 00:08:54.527
So I used to have to find
them; now they come to me.

00:08:54.527 --> 00:08:55.969
I've got two platforms to work on.

00:08:55.969 --> 00:08:57.533
This is a Shark Tank deal.

00:08:57.533 --> 00:08:59.783
So Oprah has a hairdresser.

00:09:00.783 --> 00:09:02.509
Interesting guy.

00:09:02.509 --> 00:09:04.009
His hobby was tea.

00:09:07.030 --> 00:09:08.590
This tea you drink.

00:09:08.590 --> 00:09:10.190
And he became what's called a nose.

00:09:10.190 --> 00:09:12.293
He had a certain skill to be able to smell

00:09:12.293 --> 00:09:14.800
(mumbles) teas and blend them.

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:17.305
One day he gives, a couple of years ago,

00:09:17.305 --> 00:09:19.830
a cup of tea to Oprah
while he's doing her hair.

00:09:19.830 --> 00:09:22.171
She sips it and says this
is the best tea I ever had.

00:09:22.171 --> 00:09:23.277
She goes on the show a half an hour later

00:09:23.277 --> 00:09:25.504
and says this tea called Talbott Teas

00:09:25.504 --> 00:09:27.600
is the best tea I've ever had.

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:30.931
He gets orders the next day for $500,000.

00:09:30.931 --> 00:09:32.344
You know the Oprah effect;
you've heard of it, right?

00:09:32.344 --> 00:09:33.236
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:09:33.236 --> 00:09:35.044
- He can't finance that.

00:09:35.044 --> 00:09:39.657
So he comes on Shark Tank and
he's looking for $250,000.

00:09:39.657 --> 00:09:42.370
I agree to buy 35% of
the company for that.

00:09:42.370 --> 00:09:44.135
I love tea.
It's so simple.

00:09:44.135 --> 00:09:46.605
Gets on Oprah, 500,000
orders, Neiman Marcus,

00:09:46.605 --> 00:09:48.789
all kinds of individuals
ordering on the Internet.

00:09:48.789 --> 00:09:52.561
Simple.
Tea, orders, cash flow, right?

00:09:52.561 --> 00:09:55.602
While we're in the
middle of doing the deal,

00:09:55.602 --> 00:09:58.194
the show airs, and Jamba
Juice, a public company,

00:09:58.194 --> 00:09:59.966
says we want to be in the tea business,

00:09:59.966 --> 00:10:01.729
we love this story, we love the brand,

00:10:01.729 --> 00:10:03.212
I want to buy the company.

00:10:03.212 --> 00:10:05.105
I haven't even written the check yet.

00:10:05.105 --> 00:10:06.915
And bang, the deal's done.

00:10:06.915 --> 00:10:08.104
- [Interviewer] Wow.

00:10:08.104 --> 00:10:09.461
- Yes, wow.

00:10:09.461 --> 00:10:10.856
That's the kind of deal I like.

00:10:10.856 --> 00:10:12.127
Because it was simple.

00:10:12.127 --> 00:10:13.771
It's not a complicated tech story.

00:10:13.771 --> 00:10:15.782
It's tea.
Tea with a brand.

00:10:15.782 --> 00:10:18.253
Now you go into a Jamba Juice
anywhere in North America,

00:10:18.253 --> 00:10:21.468
that's my Talbott Teas.
- Wow.

00:10:21.468 --> 00:10:22.581
- Isn't that wonderful?

00:10:22.581 --> 00:10:24.305
- It is wonderful.
That's why you are...

00:10:24.305 --> 00:10:26.154
- That's why I am Mr. Wonderful.

00:10:26.154 --> 00:10:27.554
But you know, I look at this way.

00:10:27.554 --> 00:10:29.783
The simple deals are the
ones you make money on.

00:10:29.783 --> 00:10:31.178
- [Interviewer] Okay.

00:10:31.178 --> 00:10:33.152
- So I take that cash back.
I redeploy it.

00:10:33.152 --> 00:10:36.194
So I like simple stories with
revenue attached to them.

00:10:36.194 --> 00:10:39.317
You know the high-tech stuff,
I came out of that market.

00:10:39.317 --> 00:10:41.497
It's so complicated and
so much can go wrong,

00:10:41.497 --> 00:10:45.664
so I just love deals where they
see an easy path to revenue.

00:10:47.377 --> 00:10:48.733
- So thank you guys so much for watching.

00:10:48.733 --> 00:10:50.913
I'd love to know what did
you learn from this video,

00:10:50.913 --> 00:10:53.673
what clip spoke to you
the most, and what are you

00:10:53.673 --> 00:10:55.975
going to take from these
clips and immediately apply

